t me  just tell you as long as he can point to  the fact that that police department did  not have a firmly established  intervention protocol with actual  measures that a fellow officer has to  take when they see another colleague or  officer using illegal unwise or  excessive tactics if they don't have  that in place at least he asked  questions of the other officer even a  superior one as to whether or not they  should ease up or do something different  he wasn't completely passive in the  process  now on the other hand he could have  maybe done a little bit more other than  just passively asked they should have an  established code word that lets fellow  officers at the scene know for example  that bad tactics are being used if that  doesn't work then i guess the question  is are they trying to just step up and  just push that officer off their their  victim we'll see yeah if it's not in the  books now i'm sure it's going to be in  the books for the police department  going forward terry do you think any of  the officers would have been better off  pointing the finger at each other and  why do you think they didn't  i think it makes far more sense for them  not to point their finger at each other  because that could really backfire if  you think about it i think the tactic  that they took is the better tactic they  basically said look we did what we were  trained to do as philip was mentioning  and if anything they didn't train us  well enough because we did everything we  were told  and basically i think each of them also  has their own independent argument of  course tao basically said that wasn't  his job he was watching the crowd and  lane is arguing that he did try to  intervene he did try to say roll over  and i think king probably has the more  difficult argument but he too was in a  position where he was following order so  i think it's better for all of them to  have the same story stick to the story  assert their individual defenses and  then basically try to win that way all  right we'll see how it goes as this is  kind of wrapping up in this case thank  you both and thank you for joining us  here on long crime daily we'll see you  next time as we discuss justice in  america  [Music]  you
 

